WHY THE VERDICT ALONE STOPPED BEING ENOUGH. The self-proof once "improved"
to rc 3 while its sanitizer-credited flows collapsed 2,658 -> 0: the
credited evidence had been minted by tests all along, and the verdict
machinery — correct at every step — was confirming claims over sources that
could no longer mint a single flow. A verdict-only snapshot waves exactly
that through.

SNAPSHOT v2 (docs/hypergumbo.claims.expected.json): per claim, the verdict
PLUS the sorted caveat kinds PLUS a credited-flows bucket (zero/nonzero).
The bucket is deliberate: exact counts move with every analyzer
improvement, but nonzero -> zero is the vacuousness signal and now fails
the gate. Comparison logic extracted from check-self-claims' heredoc to
scripts/check-self-claims-drift, pinned by
tests/test_check_self_claims_drift.py (the INV-zuhig shape is a named
case: verdict unchanged + credited collapse => exit 1, REGRESSED).

PER-PR ARM (ci.yml self-claims-gate): path-filtered on the claim surface —
claims yaml + snapshot, self-catalog, overlays, shipped io/taint
catalogues, pyproject deps, and the gate scripts themselves. The install
mirrors full-suite's self-tree-validation job exactly: the gate's verdicts
depend on which grammars are present, so both arms must analyze the same
language set or they disagree on identical trees. Analyzer .py changes
stay on the twice-daily full-suite cadence — a ~10-minute self-analysis on
every core PR was judged too heavy; that is a scoping decision, recorded
as such. This PR touches the surface itself, so the job's first live run
is on this PR.

